      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's focus on the chemistry, shall we?\n\nAs I was saying, the Maillard reaction involves the reaction between amino acids and reducing sugars. The amino acids, such as asparagine and glutamine, react with the reducing sugars, like glucose and fructose, to form new compounds. These compounds are responsible for the characteristic flavors, aromas, and colors of the Maillard reaction.\n\nOne of the key compounds formed during the Maillard reaction is melanoidin, a brown pigment that's responsible for the darkening of the bread crust. Melanoidin is formed through the reaction between amino acids and reducing sugars, and it's a bit like a treasure chest of flavors and aromas, with each compound contributing to the overall character of the bread.\n\nNow, when it comes to the temperatures involved, you'll want to look out for the magic numbers between 140°C to 180°C (284°F to 356°F). This is where the Maillard reaction really starts to kick in, and you'll see the bread crust transform from a pale, doughy color to a golden brown, crispy delight. At around 140°C, you'll start to see the formation",
